WEBVTT

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:13.320
And the more we discover about our planet, the stranger it gets.

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:21.080
Every day, news stories reach us, stories that surprise us...

00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:24.440
What is THAT?

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:26.080
..Shock us...

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:26.840
Whoa!

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:27.720
..Sometimes even scare us.

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:32.000
Oh, my God!

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:40.240
We've scoured the globe to bring you the most curious creatures... ..the most extraordinary people...

00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:45.040
I can stick almost anything to my skin without no glue.

00:00:44.200 --> 00:01:05.640
..And the most bizarre behaviour... ..using eyewitness accounts and expert opinion to explore a weird world... ..of unexplained underwater blobs... ..flying goats... ..and glow-in-the-dark fish.

00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:14.080
We examine the evidence, test the theories... ..to work out what on earth is going on.

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:41.320
In this episode, we'll explore an extraordinary relationship...

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:53.080
..reveal prehistoric creatures that could come back from the dead and find out why Spain is under attack from space balls.

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:57.440
It could be capable to destroy a house.

00:01:56.680 --> 00:02:14.200
But first to America, and to the coast of California, where, in February 2016, a gourmet restaurant became famous for a very unexpected guest.

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:24.240
I came in, and yes, she was there, in booth number 65, which is the best table in the restaurant, right on the water.

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:31.640
To Chef Bernard's surprise, a tiny sea lion pup was sitting, waiting for service.

00:02:30.920 --> 00:02:37.480
I've been here for 21 years and I've never seen something like this.

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:41.680
He named her Marina.

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:45.600
And stranger still, she wasn't a one-off.

00:02:44.680 --> 00:02:51.400
This sea lion pup turned up on the streets of San Francisco.

00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:54.280
Another was found in a toilet.

00:02:53.360 --> 00:03:03.080
Whilst this one was caught on CCTV indulging in a spot of shopping.

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:13.000
Up and down the coast, lots of these animals were giving up on the ocean and moving inland.

00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:13.400
Strange behaviour indeed.

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:18.920
And sea lions weren't the only animals behaving oddly on the California coast.

00:03:17.960 --> 00:03:20.400
Check this out.

00:03:19.640 --> 00:03:28.840
During 2015 and 16, millions of pelagic red crabs washed up like a crimson tide.

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:37.040
And deadly venomous sea snakes that are usually only found in the tropics littered the state's beaches.

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:48.160
But why were these marine creatures suddenly turning terrestrial?

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:53.160
Well, Chef Bernard's uninvited guest could give us a clue.

00:03:52.240 --> 00:04:05.880
By seeking out a seafood restaurant, Marina was smarter than the other sea lion pups because they all had one thing in common... ..protruding ribs, glassy eyes.

00:04:06.440 --> 00:04:08.440
These pups were starving.

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:20.000
Marina was just one of over 6,500 skinny pups rescued across the state.

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:22.880
The pups that are coming in are extremely emaciated.

00:04:21.959 --> 00:04:32.360
A lot of our pups come into us about six months old, and they're coming in just barely over birth weight.

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:41.960
What was causing these starving pups to come ashore in their thousands?

00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:48.440
Well, there's one last unexpected piece to this puzzle.

00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:58.760
Just a week before Marina showed up at Chef Bernard's restaurant, something truly extraordinary made a sudden appearance just 15km down the coast.

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:00.880
Wow!

00:04:59.960 --> 00:05:02.160
Look at that.

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:08.480
As the tide falls back along the beach in Coronado, a piece of the past is unveiled.

00:05:07.640 --> 00:05:13.000
You're looking at the remains of the 300-foot long SS Monte Carlo.

00:05:12.560 --> 00:05:20.280
But the SS Monte Carlo sank back in 1937.

00:05:19.400 --> 00:05:20.880
So where had this wreck suddenly appeared from?

00:05:20.040 --> 00:05:29.560
And how can it explain the sea snakes, crab invasion and sea lions starving to death?

00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:47.360
Well, all these bizarre events were just symptoms of something much bigger and much stranger.

00:05:47.080 --> 00:06:00.240
2015-'16 was what's known as an El Nino Year - a global weather event powerful enough to affect the whole planet.

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:14.400
Normally, trade winds in the Pacific blow from east to west, dragging warm surface waters towards Indonesia and Northeast Australia.

00:06:14.320 --> 00:06:21.360
Meanwhile, deeper colder waters in the east rise to the surface.

00:06:20.480 --> 00:06:30.520
But every few years, the trade winds weaken and can't change, bringing in warmer water to the West Coast of America.

00:06:30.440 --> 00:06:38.600
And it's these unusually warm waters caused by El Nino that explain our Californian conundrum.

00:06:38.800 --> 00:06:50.360
They triggered severe storms that washed away tonnes of sand and exposed the long-lost wreck of the SS Monte Carlo.

00:06:49.400 --> 00:06:58.800
And warm currents brought sea snakes and red crabs up from the tropics.

00:06:58.920 --> 00:07:05.440
But why would a hotter ocean spell starvation for Marina and thousands of other sea lions?

00:07:04.480 --> 00:07:09.600
Well, there's still plenty of fish out there, but not in the right place.

00:07:08.760 --> 00:07:16.400
It shifted into the cooler waters, further down below or further offshore, and that's what El Nino does.

00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:25.040
El Nino brings in this really warm water current, and so, their cold-water prey move with the cold water.

00:07:25.720 --> 00:07:32.560
Adult sea lions can follow the fish offshore, but breeding mothers and pups can't.

00:07:31.600 --> 00:07:36.440
Many perished, but the lucky ones were rescued.

00:07:35.920 --> 00:07:43.560
And Chef Bernard waved a fond farewell when Marina returned to the wild.

00:07:42.680 --> 00:07:45.120
Whoo!

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:49.880
She really, really regained a lot of weight, so she went from 20lb, became 40, 45lb.

00:07:49.040 --> 00:07:55.440
So she was healthy, she was happy, she was sassy and she was like, "Hey, Chef Bernard, "time to go back in the big great blue!"

00:08:08.680 --> 00:08:19.960
So, a sea lion sauntering into a seafood restaurant was just a small, skinny symptom of the world's largest weather phenomenon.

00:08:19.080 --> 00:08:26.680
And the 2015-2016 El Nino was no ordinary El Nino.

00:08:25.960 --> 00:08:35.120
It was the strongest since the 1950s and its effects were felt far wider than the Sunshine State.

00:08:34.320 --> 00:08:38.520
Yes, this was some seriously weird weather.

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:51.240
Yes, whilst strange weather was to blame for the peculiar events in California, our next mystery was washed up by unusual currents off Brazil.

00:08:50.720 --> 00:09:03.480
Every June, 71-year-old Joao Pereira waits for his best friend, Dindim, to arrive.

00:09:02.680 --> 00:09:09.240
They haven't seen each other for months, because Dindim's been away at sea.

00:09:08.560 --> 00:09:10.720
But finally, the wait is over.

00:09:18.360 --> 00:09:22.120
Sorry, I didn't tell you, did I?

00:09:21.200 --> 00:09:23.560
Dindim's a penguin.

00:09:26.640 --> 00:09:32.440
A Magellanic penguin, to be precise.

00:09:32.320 --> 00:09:36.960
And for the past five years, Dindim and Joao have been devoted to each other.

00:09:36.160 --> 00:09:42.560
So how did man and bird become such bosom buddies?

00:09:42.600 --> 00:09:50.800
Joao's house backs onto Proveta Beach, near Rio De Janeiro.

00:09:49.880 --> 00:09:56.800
In May 2011, he found Dindim on the sand, barely moving and covered in oil.

00:09:55.920 --> 00:10:00.720
Joao looked after the penguin.

00:09:59.840 --> 00:10:03.640
He cleaned his feathers.

00:10:02.800 --> 00:10:08.440
And fed him sardines.

00:10:07.560 --> 00:10:20.600
After a few days, when he thought Dindim was strong enough to fend for himself, Joao took the penguin to a nearby island and released him into the sea.

00:10:20.360 --> 00:10:29.840
But just a few hours later, Joao heard squeaking in his backyard.

00:10:29.120 --> 00:10:46.640
Much to his surprise, the penguin was back, and made himself at home with Joao and his wife.

00:10:52.080 --> 00:10:56.840
Dindim and Joao were inseparable for 11 months.

00:10:57.200 --> 00:11:00.240
But then the penguin suddenly left.

00:10:59.600 --> 00:11:08.840
Perhaps his instincts had kicked in and he'd gone to find his own kind.

00:11:07.960 --> 00:11:18.920
Magellanic penguins live in the sea off of South America, and sometimes they'll venture as far north as Rio here.

00:11:18.160 --> 00:11:24.640
But once a year they have to head back south to Patagonia to breed.

00:11:23.800 --> 00:11:37.120
So Joao could only imagine that Dindim, fully restored to health, had hopped down off of the sofa, taken to the sea and swum south to be reunited with some of his fellow penguins.

00:11:36.520 --> 00:11:46.840
But what's incredible is that a few months later, much to Joao's delight, Dindim returned.

00:11:46.000 --> 00:11:52.160
And the same thing happens every year.

00:11:51.320 --> 00:12:01.680
Dindim heads out to sea for a few months to feed, but he always returns to Joao.

00:12:01.120 --> 00:12:04.600
So what's going on?

00:12:03.720 --> 00:12:12.240
Why does Dindim keep coming back to Joao rather than living with his own species?

00:12:11.440 --> 00:12:20.640
Well, the answer may lie in the fact that the penguin was only about a year old when Joao rescued him.

00:12:19.760 --> 00:12:28.160
It could be that Dindim has imprinted upon Joao to the extent that he sees Joao as his parent.

00:12:27.360 --> 00:12:31.880
You see, when birds hatch, they do so with little sense of identity.

00:12:31.120 --> 00:12:37.400
They have to look around them to see what they are and how to behave.

00:12:36.520 --> 00:12:46.560
And sometimes, if the first thing they see is a human, they bond to it for life.

00:12:46.200 --> 00:12:53.480
So perhaps this special relationship is due to some kind of delayed imprinting.

00:12:53.320 --> 00:12:54.560
A case of mistaken identity.

00:12:53.800 --> 00:13:03.760
But there could be another possibility that Dindim sees Joao not as a parent, but as his partner.

00:13:03.000 --> 00:13:15.480
You see, Dindim always returns around July, which is the beginning of the penguin breeding season.

00:13:14.800 --> 00:13:24.000
Other Magellanic penguins are in Patagonia then, raising a family with their mate.

00:13:23.080 --> 00:13:28.480
So is Dindim's affectionate behaviour actually an attempt at courtship?

00:13:27.640 --> 00:13:32.720
Well, it's not quite that simple.

00:13:31.960 --> 00:13:40.880
Penguins are usually very loyal to the places that they spend their summer months.

00:13:40.160 --> 00:13:49.440
They breed in Patagonia, they usually come back to the very same beach every year and they nest in the very same hole every year with the same partner.

00:13:48.560 --> 00:13:50.160
Most of them are like that.

00:13:49.240 --> 00:14:01.440
Because Dindim spends so long with Mr Joao on Proveta Beach he probably imprinted and learned that that place is a safe place to be, and is the place he has to go during the summer months.

00:14:03.400 --> 00:14:12.480
So it seems that because Dindim spent his formative first summer with Joao, he now sees that beach as his home.

00:14:12.560 --> 00:14:21.040
Whatever the biological explanation may be, the warm fuzzy feeling between this penguin and his pal is clearly mutual.

00:14:22.040 --> 00:14:28.400
So Dindim is usually very calm and comfortable and happy around Mr Joao, and the opposite is true as well.

00:14:27.960 --> 00:14:32.440
He is in heaven when Dindim is around.

00:14:31.480 --> 00:14:33.200
I would say this is a friendship, why not?

00:14:33.080 --> 00:14:38.960
What better definition for friendship than that?

00:14:38.680 --> 00:14:44.040
Without Joao's help, Dindim surely would have died.

00:14:43.120 --> 00:14:50.040
His kind actions have earned him an unlikely new best friend.

00:14:53.800 --> 00:15:03.280
While Atlantic currents delivered this penguin to a loving new home, unusual weather revealed a puzzle in the permafrost.

00:15:02.400 --> 00:15:07.440
Siberia.

00:15:06.640 --> 00:15:12.080
Where temperatures often fall to minus-40 degrees.

00:15:11.120 --> 00:15:15.360
And the ground is frozen solid year round.

00:15:14.480 --> 00:15:24.560
But in August 2015, a freak flood in the Sakha Republic exposed a fresh patch of tundra.

00:15:23.760 --> 00:15:30.000
Scientists working in the area stumbled across a weird-looking lump.

00:15:30.840 --> 00:15:40.560
They chipped away at the block of ice...

00:15:39.680 --> 00:15:43.520
Something furry emerged.

00:15:43.640 --> 00:15:54.560
The scientists had uncovered two frozen animals, but what could they be?

00:15:54.280 --> 00:15:57.400
Wild dogs?

00:15:57.360 --> 00:15:59.640
Polar bears?

00:15:58.760 --> 00:16:00.800
No, this was something even more extraordinary.

00:16:00.120 --> 00:16:05.360
A couple of very young lion cubs.

00:16:04.560 --> 00:16:06.200
Wait a minute.

00:16:05.320 --> 00:16:15.920
Lions in Siberia, how could that be?

00:16:15.280 --> 00:16:21.280
I mean, we all know that lions roam the plains of Africa here.

00:16:21.080 --> 00:16:26.240
And that there is a small population over here in north-western India.

00:16:26.520 --> 00:16:33.720
But that's still more than 3,000 miles away from Siberia, here.

00:16:32.920 --> 00:16:37.960
So how did those cubs turn up so far from home?

00:16:37.080 --> 00:16:43.480
Well, in fact, the icy Siberian tundra was their home.

00:16:42.640 --> 00:16:46.840
But not for 10,000 years.

00:16:45.880 --> 00:16:51.960
These are baby cave lions, and they've been extinct for millennia.

00:16:51.080 --> 00:17:00.440
Cave lions were around from a little over 300,000 years ago, when we first find them in Europe.

00:16:59.480 --> 00:17:07.720
To approximately between 14,000 and 10,000 years ago was when they started to disappear from most of the range.

00:17:06.760 --> 00:17:23.839
Cave lions were about 10% bigger than modern African lions and roamed most of the Northern Hemisphere, from Alaska to eastern Russia.

00:17:23.160 --> 00:17:33.160
After detailed investigation, biologists hypothesise that the cub's mother left them in a den while she went off hunting.

00:17:32.280 --> 00:17:42.040
And then while she was away, a landslide caused the den to collapse, and the cubs perished.

00:17:41.280 --> 00:17:51.960
But if these cubs have been dead for over 10,000 years, how do they look so good for their age?

00:17:51.160 --> 00:17:54.480
Well, a simple demonstration should make that clear.

00:17:54.040 --> 00:17:56.440
But I warn you, it's not pretty.

00:17:55.640 --> 00:17:59.800
Look away if you're squeamish.

00:17:59.480 --> 00:18:09.440
Left alone at room temperature, a chicken decomposes within a week or so.

00:18:08.480 --> 00:18:09.680
Bacteria and maggots make short work of the carcass.

00:18:10.680 --> 00:18:18.200
But if we simulate the Siberian permafrost, the chicken is frozen in time.

00:18:17.240 --> 00:18:21.640
It's not just icy temperatures that keep the flesh nice and fresh, though.

00:18:22.640 --> 00:18:34.480
A combination of a lack of oxygen and no sunlight also meant the cubs were preserved for thousands of years.

00:18:33.720 --> 00:18:38.720
A chance in a million for biologists.

00:18:38.120 --> 00:18:40.680
From a paleontological standpoint, it's a hugely significant find.

00:18:39.760 --> 00:18:43.480
We very rarely get soft tissue preservation of extinct animals.

00:18:42.560 --> 00:18:51.640
Not just the skeleton, but all the soft tissue, you know, all the muscles and the brain and the fur.

00:18:50.680 --> 00:18:54.440
It's just amazing.

00:18:57.200 --> 00:19:00.480
And the soft tissue is the final twist in this Siberian story.

00:18:59.640 --> 00:19:08.600
You see, not content with merely uncovering these cubs, biologists plan to do the unthinkable.

00:19:07.800 --> 00:19:10.200
To clone the cave lion.

00:19:09.440 --> 00:19:12.080
To bring it back to life.

00:19:11.280 --> 00:19:12.520
But how?

00:19:12.480 --> 00:19:21.400
Well, science has progressed a lot since the first mammal was cloned back in 1996.

00:19:20.560 --> 00:19:27.000
'Scientists in Scotland have produced the first-ever clone 'of an adult animal.

00:19:26.200 --> 00:19:35.200
'Dolly, a seven-month-old sheep, 'was created in a laboratory using a cell from another sheep.

00:19:34.400 --> 00:19:40.440
'The new step involves taking a cell from an adult sheep 'and removing the genetic material from it.

00:19:39.560 --> 00:19:44.840
'The genes are then inserted into an empty egg cell taken from another 'sheep.

00:19:44.440 --> 00:19:53.080
The egg is then used to start a pregnancy, 'the offspring being a clone.'

00:19:54.880 --> 00:20:08.800
If intact DNA could be extracted from the cave lions then, in theory, they could be brought back from extinction.

00:20:07.920 --> 00:20:18.960
Korean biologist Hwang Woo-suk is taking tissue samples from the cubs.

00:20:18.160 --> 00:20:29.800
Russian and Korean scientists are already working on cloning ancient mammoths.

00:20:29.040 --> 00:20:37.480
And as far fetched as this Jurassic dream sounds, it might just be possible.

00:20:36.520 --> 00:20:46.720
You see, they don't need pristine DNA to try and bring back the cave lion, or, in this case, the mammoth.

00:20:45.920 --> 00:20:50.720
Let's imagine that these parts of this jigsaw represents the good mammoth DNA that they have.

00:20:49.840 --> 00:21:03.600
The problem is...they've only got half a mammoth.

00:21:02.640 --> 00:21:18.360
Even if scientists manage to create a viable embryo, that's just the first step.

00:21:19.880 --> 00:21:25.080
I think what people forget with cloning is that you need a host animal.

00:21:24.120 --> 00:21:29.080
And you need many, many replicates in order to get any sort of success.

00:21:28.400 --> 00:21:31.360
So dozens, to potentially hundreds, of surrogate mothers.

00:21:30.400 --> 00:21:36.040
Of course, if you look at modern lion populations, they are plummeting.

00:21:35.160 --> 00:21:38.560
Just in the last few decades, the lion populations have been cut almost in half.

00:21:37.600 --> 00:21:44.240
So the amount of resources that would go into cloning the cave lion, I think, could be much better spent on saving the lions that are around today.

00:21:48.480 --> 00:21:58.280
The possibility of cloning animals like cave lions and mammoths is undeniably exciting.

00:21:57.360 --> 00:22:06.440
But it's my duty to tell you that, at the moment, a real-life Jurassic Park is still some way off.

00:22:05.480 --> 00:22:16.840
So, whilst El Nino forced starving sea lions inland, usual currents carried this Patagonian penguin to Rio.

00:22:16.040 --> 00:22:23.080
And a freak flood gave new life to prehistoric lion cubs.

00:22:24.520 --> 00:22:31.440
When weird weather strikes, it can transport animals into some very unlikely places.

00:22:30.640 --> 00:22:33.120
Coming up...

00:22:32.360 --> 00:22:42.360
A selection of superpowers... an exceptionally alluring insect... and an invader destroying houses in the suburbs.

00:22:41.440 --> 00:22:52.560
But first, we discover how one Scottish woman's remarkable sense of smell could change the future of medicine.

00:22:51.680 --> 00:22:57.160
I've always smelt things.

00:22:56.360 --> 00:23:04.160
If I can help it, I don't go in to cake shops, it's a smile that I don't particularly like.

00:23:08.360 --> 00:23:13.480
Joy Milne's world is dominated by scent.

00:23:12.840 --> 00:23:19.480
And her exceptionally sensitive nose affects the way she perceives people.

00:23:18.520 --> 00:23:21.920
People just don't smell, they have layers of smell.

00:23:20.960 --> 00:23:30.160
They've got a perfume or a spray on, but then they've got clothes which they have then washed in a fabric softener.

00:23:29.240 --> 00:23:33.080
Then they've got their body smells.

00:23:32.280 --> 00:23:37.880
So, for me, a person has at least two or three different smells.

00:23:38.560 --> 00:23:42.640
To Joy, every person has a distinct odour.

00:23:41.760 --> 00:23:45.840
Including her husband, Les.

00:23:44.960 --> 00:23:47.320
So she noticed straightaway when his scent suddenly changed.

00:23:46.560 --> 00:23:57.480
'Out of the blue,' I could smell this very musky, very heavy smell on him.

00:23:56.560 --> 00:24:01.680
Joy worked as a nurse, while Les was an anaesthetist.

00:24:01.360 --> 00:24:10.680
She assumed that his unpleasant body odour was down to working long hours in theatre.

00:24:09.880 --> 00:24:15.400
I did say to him, "I'm sorry, but you're not, you know, washing enough."

00:24:14.440 --> 00:24:21.800
And it became quite a contentious sort of thing, because he was showering.

00:24:21.440 --> 00:24:29.840
Over the next few years, Joy noticed that Les's musky scent became stronger.

00:24:28.960 --> 00:24:33.120
And he developed other problems.

00:24:32.320 --> 00:24:39.760
Les was a keen sportsman, but his coordination began to falter.

00:24:39.160 --> 00:24:46.840
We'd play squash until our late 30s, things like he was missing the ball.

00:24:45.960 --> 00:24:54.720
He couldn't keep up in the game, because usually he beat me no bother at all.

00:24:54.520 --> 00:25:00.360
Les's personality changed too.

00:25:00.560 --> 00:25:09.000
He was generally known to be an extremely laid-back person.

00:25:08.200 --> 00:25:11.600
He became irritable about things.

00:25:10.760 --> 00:25:14.680
He became aggressive, sometimes.

00:25:13.720 --> 00:25:23.440
It's very strange living with somebody that you've known since he was 16, change quite a bit in the gap of ten years.

00:25:22.680 --> 00:25:29.200
So what could be causing these worrying mental and physical symptoms?

00:25:28.600 --> 00:25:40.480
Les was eventually diagnosed with Parkinson's disease, a progressive disorder of the nervous system, but not a condition that had ever before been associated with a change in body odour.

00:25:39.800 --> 00:25:45.880
So could there possibly be a connection here?

00:25:45.000 --> 00:25:53.600
When Joy went to a Parkinson's support group, she made a sensational discovery.

00:25:52.720 --> 00:25:54.640
And she couldn't wait to tell her husband.

00:25:53.840 --> 00:26:00.960
I said to him, "These people smell the same as you."

00:26:00.080 --> 00:26:03.080
And he said, "What are you talking about?"

00:26:02.320 --> 00:26:09.960
And I said, "Those people smell exactly the same as you."

00:26:11.080 --> 00:26:17.600
Joy seems to have an extraordinary ability to sniff out Parkinson's.

00:26:16.800 --> 00:26:23.280
As medical professionals, Joy and Les realised that this could be ground-breaking.

00:26:22.320 --> 00:26:34.480
There's currently no simple way to diagnose Parkinson's and Joy's nose could hold the key to developing a test.

00:26:33.800 --> 00:26:40.320
So Joy approached scientists working on the disease and told them about her strange ability.

00:26:39.400 --> 00:26:46.840
Together, we devised a method to try and see if we could understand what it was, what the smell was.

00:26:46.040 --> 00:26:56.560
We did a control experiment where we had people suffering from Parkinson's, fairly late-stage on, and people who weren't.

00:26:55.680 --> 00:26:57.880
We made them wear T-shirts.

00:26:56.920 --> 00:27:03.520
And we then cut the T-shirts up and put them in bags so that they were anonymous and took them to Joy to smell.

00:27:02.720 --> 00:27:10.000
And Joy was extremely good at identifying the people who had Parkinson's.

00:27:09.360 --> 00:27:15.720
Joy got all but one sample correct.

00:27:14.760 --> 00:27:19.360
She thought one of the people without Parkinson's, in fact, had the disease.

00:27:18.400 --> 00:27:22.560
Perhaps her olfactory powers weren't foolproof after all.

00:27:21.720 --> 00:27:27.120
But then, something incredible happened.

00:27:26.400 --> 00:27:33.960
That person, about a year afterwards, was diagnosed with Parkinson's disease.

00:27:33.000 --> 00:27:37.480
Joy had been right all along.

00:27:36.640 --> 00:27:49.040
What is mind-blowing here is that she could detect Parkinson's simply using her nose - well before any doctor could.

00:27:48.240 --> 00:27:49.800
So what is it that Joy can smell?

00:27:49.240 --> 00:27:53.440
It's all down to sebum.

00:27:52.680 --> 00:27:58.560
That oily substance that coats our skin.

00:27:57.840 --> 00:28:00.680
It's made up of thousands of different ingredients.

00:28:00.680 --> 00:28:06.600
We identified approximately 9,000 unique molecules.

00:28:05.640 --> 00:28:12.240
What we need to now do is to find out what the differences are between a healthy person and someone with Parkinson's.

00:28:11.320 --> 00:28:24.040
Once the team have identified which chemicals cause the distinctive Parkinson's smell, they can develop a test to find those compounds rather than relying on Joy's nose.

00:28:23.320 --> 00:28:31.600
We'll be able to diagnose this disease at an early stage, before some of the devastating symptoms have started.

00:28:30.760 --> 00:28:37.960
And an early diagnosis is crucial to managing this condition effectively.

00:28:39.560 --> 00:28:42.640
But there's something even more exciting.

00:28:41.760 --> 00:28:46.160
The smell that Joy first discovered could also help develop new medicines.

00:28:46.240 --> 00:29:02.720
'Because it happens so early,' because there's a change so early on in the start of the disease, it may tell us something really crucial about the beginning stage of the disease, which will open the door to new treatments and a better understanding of how it progresses.

00:29:01.840 --> 00:29:07.760
Sadly, Les died in 2015.

00:29:06.960 --> 00:29:16.400
But Joy is determined to use her finely-tuned sense of smell to help others and raise awareness of the early symptoms of Parkinson's.

00:29:18.080 --> 00:29:21.760
You're given what you're given in life.

00:29:20.800 --> 00:29:27.920
I've had this smell, I've married this person, they've then got Parkinson's.

00:29:27.160 --> 00:29:29.960
And I've lived through it.

00:29:29.120 --> 00:29:34.760
I don't want other people to have that, that problem.

00:29:34.080 --> 00:29:35.680
It's horrendous.

00:29:34.840 --> 00:29:37.240
It is.

00:29:36.400 --> 00:29:37.680
It really is.

00:29:37.000 --> 00:29:41.880
And I want that early diagnosis.

00:29:41.960 --> 00:29:46.520
Joy's fortitude through such difficult times is incredible in itself.

00:29:45.720 --> 00:29:59.000
But the fact that she noticed a change in her husband's smell and related it directly to his disease could lead to massive leaps forward in medical science.

00:29:58.400 --> 00:30:06.920
You know, sometimes it's just the quirky little things in biology - in this case, an extraordinary sense of smell - which leads to such rapid progress.

00:30:12.960 --> 00:30:26.120
Whilst Joy's super-sense could help fight Parkinson's disease, our next weird tale features an animal with a super-secret weapon.

00:30:25.160 --> 00:30:37.520
The remote Zagros Mountains in western Iran - home to an animal found nowhere else on earth.

00:30:36.760 --> 00:30:39.920
A strange spiderlike creature.

00:30:39.800 --> 00:30:52.960
It's the height of the breeding season, and birds are desperately trying to find enough food for their growing chicks.

00:30:52.160 --> 00:30:55.840
So this looks like a perfect, juicy meal.

00:30:55.960 --> 00:31:02.000
But on this occasion, it's the bird that becomes dinner, for a perfectly camouflaged viper.

00:31:05.280 --> 00:31:19.200
And it looks like the snake is joining forces with the creepy-crawly.

00:31:18.440 --> 00:31:21.800
That is astonishing.

00:31:20.840 --> 00:31:24.960
What is that weird spider thing?

00:31:24.120 --> 00:31:27.280
What is it doing?

00:31:26.560 --> 00:31:30.160
Could it really be helping the snake to catch its prey?

00:31:29.480 --> 00:31:31.280
Well, in a sense, it is.

00:31:30.880 --> 00:31:35.720
But the truth is much, much more bizarre than that.

00:31:36.600 --> 00:31:42.600
Have a closer look.

00:31:44.280 --> 00:31:49.680
Look, that creepy-crawly appears to be clinging on to the snake's tail.

00:31:49.000 --> 00:32:01.920
Reptile expert Steven Anderson was mystified when he first saw a specimen in the Chicago Field Museum over 40 years ago.

00:32:01.000 --> 00:32:07.320
I was there to identify a collection of reptiles from Iran.

00:32:06.360 --> 00:32:09.000
I happened to open a bottle.

00:32:08.040 --> 00:32:13.680
I pulled the snake out and looked at it, and I didn't know what to think of it at that point.

00:32:12.720 --> 00:32:17.280
On closer inspection, Steven got a shock.

00:32:16.840 --> 00:32:26.720
I was very startled to see that it was actually an ornamentation of the tail itself.

00:32:26.800 --> 00:32:31.280
Yes, this thing was actually part of the snake.

00:32:30.880 --> 00:32:34.720
But what was it?

00:32:34.160 --> 00:32:45.320
With just a single preserved specimen, it was impossible to work out whether it was just a ghoulish anomaly or a whole new species.

00:32:45.480 --> 00:32:54.360
For four decades, the mystery remained unsolved.

00:32:53.480 --> 00:32:57.080
Until, in 2014, when the snake was filmed in the wild.

00:32:56.240 --> 00:33:01.680
And the truth was finally revealed.

00:33:09.520 --> 00:33:15.480
That strange structure is a lure, used to entice prey.

00:33:14.600 --> 00:33:27.560
It moves its tail back and forth along the ground, and because of the strange appendage, these elongated scales look like legs when it moves, like a spider moving around.

00:33:26.960 --> 00:33:32.240
And the end of the tail, the last two scales, form what looks like the body of this creature.

00:33:31.280 --> 00:33:32.920
This one seems to be very attractive to birds.

00:33:41.040 --> 00:33:50.640
This surreal snake was new to science, and imaginatively named... ..the spider-tailed pit viper.

00:33:52.960 --> 00:33:58.040
Other animals use a similar strategy.

00:33:57.080 --> 00:34:04.920
A humpbacked anglerfish attracts prey with its built-in fishing rod.

00:34:04.200 --> 00:34:10.040
And this turtle's wormlike tongue fascinates its unsuspecting victim.

00:34:09.360 --> 00:34:10.639
It's a very useful trick.

00:34:09.679 --> 00:34:11.520
Just sit still and tempt your dinner to within striking distance.

00:34:18.760 --> 00:34:24.760
And the viper attacks within two-tenths of a second.

00:34:26.199 --> 00:34:29.520
Then it just waits for its venom to take effect.

00:34:36.280 --> 00:34:46.320
That tantalising tail is THE most elaborate lure in the reptile world.

00:34:45.360 --> 00:34:49.800
That is one sneaky snake.

00:34:52.880 --> 00:35:02.840
And whilst I recognise that a snake with a tail like a spider is the stuff of nightmares to many people, for me, this thing is a triumph of evolution.

00:35:02.040 --> 00:35:04.640
It's remarkable.

00:35:04.240 --> 00:35:16.400
From a predator that hides in plain sight, we now travel to Florida, where intruders are lurking in the undergrowth.

00:35:16.320 --> 00:35:18.080
Miami.

00:35:17.160 --> 00:35:20.040
Glamorous.

00:35:19.120 --> 00:35:20.640
Cool.

00:35:20.040 --> 00:35:26.160
Playground of the rich and beautiful.

00:35:25.200 --> 00:35:31.160
But a recent invasion is distressing local residents.

00:35:30.320 --> 00:35:32.240
Oh, my God.

00:35:31.360 --> 00:35:33.120
I will never go out.

00:35:32.280 --> 00:35:37.000
I will not let my kids go outside and play.

00:35:36.640 --> 00:35:40.800
We didn't know what they were in the beginning.

00:35:39.920 --> 00:35:45.440
They would start climbing up the trees and just seemed to stay there.

00:35:44.520 --> 00:35:45.640
We began to see...hundreds.

00:35:44.760 --> 00:35:52.120
Properties are being overrun by alien creatures.

00:35:51.320 --> 00:35:53.440
I found one the size of my hand.

00:35:52.480 --> 00:35:56.880
So what's the cause of all of this anxiety?

00:35:56.160 --> 00:35:58.480
They are big.

00:35:58.160 --> 00:36:02.640
There are slimy and a lot of people think they're downright disgusting.

00:36:01.800 --> 00:36:03.440
Snails.

00:36:03.760 --> 00:36:05.440
Large snails.

00:36:04.680 --> 00:36:08.320
They're everywhere.

00:36:07.600 --> 00:36:14.200
I know what you're thinking, have the residents of Miami gone completely crazy?

00:36:13.480 --> 00:36:19.040
I mean, every garden has slugs and snails, doesn't it?

00:36:18.160 --> 00:36:20.000
Well, it might.

00:36:19.040 --> 00:36:20.320
But not snails like this one.

00:36:19.360 --> 00:36:23.920
You see, this is a giant African land snail.

00:36:23.120 --> 00:36:26.480
They can grow to up to 20 centimetres.

00:36:25.600 --> 00:36:29.960
They can live for up to nine years.

00:36:30.080 --> 00:36:33.120
These are monster snails.

00:36:32.360 --> 00:36:35.880
And they are causing panic because they don't just munch on a few garden plants.

00:36:36.680 --> 00:36:40.840
They'll eat over 500 different crops.

00:36:39.880 --> 00:36:43.880
They'll even devour people's homes.

00:36:42.920 --> 00:36:46.120
Yes, really.

00:36:45.400 --> 00:36:51.240
They're eating the concrete.

00:36:50.880 --> 00:37:01.720
Because they're getting calcium out of that to help build their shells stronger and bigger.

00:37:01.040 --> 00:37:15.000
But what's most worrying is that in Florida, they may carry a type of meningitis that can be passed on to humans via their slime.

00:37:14.160 --> 00:37:20.680
So these snails are a menace to human health and to the economy.

00:37:20.280 --> 00:37:23.520
But where have they all come from?

00:37:22.680 --> 00:37:26.200
Well, they are native to Africa.

00:37:25.280 --> 00:37:32.120
No-one is completely sure how they first came to Florida, but people have been caught smuggling them into the country.

00:37:31.320 --> 00:37:37.400
A lady coming back from Nigeria last year had 12 of them hidden under her dress.

00:37:36.720 --> 00:37:45.120
A man coming back from the Philippines, and he had one giant African snail in each one of his suit pockets.

00:37:44.200 --> 00:37:55.560
However they arrived, with plenty of lush vegetation and no natural predators, the invading snail population has boomed.

00:37:54.600 --> 00:37:56.360
Meet Harry the snail.

00:37:55.480 --> 00:38:02.560
Or maybe Harriet the snail, You see, it's difficult to ascribe a name to a snail, because they are hermaphrodite.

00:38:01.680 --> 00:38:08.080
They have both male and female reproductive apparatus.

00:38:07.320 --> 00:38:08.640
This is a real benefit to them.

00:38:07.720 --> 00:38:14.920
They don't have to go through that rigmarole of finding and then charming a mate.

00:38:14.160 --> 00:38:16.400
They can simply get on with reproduction.

00:38:15.520 --> 00:38:16.600
And they do.

00:38:15.680 --> 00:38:23.160
They can produce up to 1,000 eggs a year.

00:38:22.520 --> 00:38:23.720
I mean, you do the maths.

00:38:23.320 --> 00:38:25.840
These things are unstoppable.

00:38:24.920 --> 00:38:36.760
So the risk of disease and downright destruction means that local authorities are under pressure to eradicate these slimy intruders.

00:38:36.040 --> 00:38:44.400
We created a giant African land snail incident command.

00:38:43.480 --> 00:38:50.920
We're doing a lot of outreach in schools, at events, we do billboards, radio ads and television ads.

00:38:50.480 --> 00:38:57.080
The authorities rely on people ringing in with their sightings.

00:38:57.400 --> 00:39:03.520
I started seeing signs on the back of trucks which said, "Call the state of Florida."

00:39:02.800 --> 00:39:10.040
As soon as I saw this meningitis... ..that scared me to death.

00:39:12.400 --> 00:39:20.960
They came several times a week, and they would pick up hundreds of them and put them in plastic bags.

00:39:20.000 --> 00:39:21.360
And, you know, take them away.

00:39:20.400 --> 00:39:23.200
The snail squad seem to be getting on top of the slow-mo swarm.

00:39:24.200 --> 00:39:31.800
We've collected over 162,000 giant African land snails.

00:39:32.080 --> 00:39:44.360
But if these snails can lay 100 eggs at a time, to get on top of the problem, the authorities are going to need to find every single last one of them.

00:39:43.520 --> 00:39:50.200
And until recently, that's been an almost impossible task.

00:39:49.440 --> 00:39:54.440
But now, the authorities have a secret weapon.

00:39:53.600 --> 00:40:00.480
Meet Sierra.

00:39:59.560 --> 00:40:01.480
Come on, let's go.

00:40:00.640 --> 00:40:07.400
We use canines because they can get to places where humans can't.

00:40:06.560 --> 00:40:13.600
They can smell that snail and go after it and alert us that there are giant African snails there.

00:40:12.640 --> 00:40:14.120
Yeah!

00:40:13.360 --> 00:40:15.000
Good girl, babe.

00:40:14.120 --> 00:40:15.880
You found it.

00:40:15.080 --> 00:40:17.440
Good girl.

00:40:16.520 --> 00:40:17.960
Good girl.

00:40:20.920 --> 00:40:28.160
In Shannon's garden, the snail squad's efforts seem to be working.

00:40:27.360 --> 00:40:33.000
It's been amazingly successful because for months, I have not seen a snail.

00:40:32.160 --> 00:40:38.360
But Mark and his team must remain vigilant.

00:40:37.520 --> 00:40:48.840
Just the other day we found a 4.8 incher, which is an adult, which means that there are children, or neonates that are out there in the wild.

00:40:48.000 --> 00:40:53.000
We have to keep looking for them.

00:40:54.720 --> 00:41:02.920
So the snail squad's efforts and their search for these marauding molluscs is set to continue.

00:41:02.320 --> 00:41:11.840
And Sierra's sniffing services are going to be needed for a while yet.

00:41:13.160 --> 00:41:27.320
From a super-sensitive nose that can sniff out Parkinson's disease and a snake with a hidden weapon... ..to a super-sized house-eating snail, there's no doubt that nature's superpowers can lead to some truly bizarre consequences.

00:41:31.120 --> 00:41:33.600
Coming up...

00:41:34.680 --> 00:41:40.480
A strange glowing slime.

00:41:40.160 --> 00:41:45.280
And alien objects that fell from the sky.

00:41:44.840 --> 00:41:53.800
But first, we head to the holiday resort of Fethiye on the Turkish coast.

00:41:53.240 --> 00:42:07.800
On the 9th of July 2015, dive instructor Lutfu Tanriover jumped into the calm blue waters of the Mediterranean, something he'd done hundreds of times before.

00:42:07.840 --> 00:42:11.720
I have been diving the area for eight years.

00:42:10.880 --> 00:42:15.600
And at least once a week we are going to that dive site.

00:42:14.760 --> 00:42:24.360
Little did he know what he'd find under the waves this time.

00:42:23.520 --> 00:42:24.840
A blob of baffling proportions.

00:42:26.720 --> 00:42:34.240
It was three, four metres wide.

00:42:33.280 --> 00:42:39.080
And drifting about 22 metres in the middle of the water.

00:42:38.240 --> 00:42:44.720
It was a very, very big bubble.

00:42:43.840 --> 00:42:50.200
We were all fascinated by it because nobody had ever seen something like that before.

00:42:49.400 --> 00:42:53.680
I decided to call it The Thing.

00:42:53.120 --> 00:43:06.000
Lutfu posted this footage of The Thing online, where it grabbed the attention of marine biologist Steve Haddock.

00:43:05.120 --> 00:43:07.960
So what did he think this bewildering blob could be?

00:43:07.160 --> 00:43:12.080
When you see something like that, there's only a few things that it might potentially be.

00:43:11.360 --> 00:43:16.640
One of the things is a pyrosome.

00:43:15.880 --> 00:43:26.960
This is a colony of organisms that can form either a small tube or, actually, one species can form very, very large tubes that divers can actually swim inside of.

00:43:26.000 --> 00:43:32.120
But the thing that Lutfu filmed was a ball.

00:43:32.960 --> 00:43:34.320
Not a tube.

00:43:33.480 --> 00:43:40.120
So if it wasn't a colony of individual creatures, what was this awe-inspiring orb?

00:43:39.440 --> 00:43:42.880
And where had it come from?

00:43:41.960 --> 00:43:49.120
The secret to the blob's source lay inside the sphere.

00:43:48.240 --> 00:43:49.640
You see, initially, it looked translucent.

00:43:48.840 --> 00:43:50.040
See-through.

00:43:49.080 --> 00:43:52.800
But then, under closer inspection, Lutfu could see something inside.

00:43:56.520 --> 00:44:10.000
When you go next to it, we put the torches in it, and then we saw with the torches, we have seen so many particles in it.

00:44:09.240 --> 00:44:13.960
These particles provided a vital clue.

00:44:13.600 --> 00:44:19.360
You see, this isn't a single giant blob at all.

00:44:18.440 --> 00:44:19.720
But millions of tiny individual spheres.

00:44:18.840 --> 00:44:22.680
Squid eggs.

00:44:23.200 --> 00:44:34.040
Instead of having them individually, they're like little snacks for other organisms to live on, they embed them in a giant gelatinous mass.

00:44:35.560 --> 00:44:42.760
It's a strategy that certain species of squid use in the open ocean where there are lots of hungry mouths around.

00:44:41.800 --> 00:44:47.840
Predators could come from any direction, and you have very few ways to hide or secure your eggs.

00:44:46.880 --> 00:44:50.520
But if you put them in this mass, it's large enough, it excludes all the predators that are smaller than that.

00:44:52.240 --> 00:45:04.680
This enormous egg mass is the squid's way of maximising its offspring's chances of survival.

00:45:05.160 --> 00:45:11.080
Blobs like this are rarely seen, they last just a few days before breaking up.

00:45:10.200 --> 00:45:13.320
Then each baby squid will face the world alone.

00:45:14.120 --> 00:45:18.240
But there's one puzzle left.

00:45:17.320 --> 00:45:24.600
So how does a relatively small squid produce something so huge?

00:45:24.000 --> 00:45:29.920
Well, its eggs start off quite small.

00:45:29.680 --> 00:45:35.040
But they expand massively in sea water.

00:45:35.120 --> 00:45:38.160
It's a bit like frog spawn on steroids.

00:45:38.040 --> 00:45:46.560
So this vast blob of jelly is the secret to a squid's success.

00:45:45.600 --> 00:45:50.160
Protecting its precious eggs from predators.

00:45:49.320 --> 00:45:57.320
Whilst this bizarre ball appeared underwater... ..our next mystery was spotted underground.

00:45:56.520 --> 00:46:09.120
In March 2016, Anthony Roberts was exploring this old slate mine in North Wales.

00:46:09.040 --> 00:46:14.400
Scoping out new areas for guided tours.

00:46:13.680 --> 00:46:21.800
His normal route in and out was flooded, so he used the emergency exit tunnels instead.

00:46:21.000 --> 00:46:25.120
On the way out, something stopped him in his tracks.

00:46:29.080 --> 00:46:35.480
A very strange, very bright luminous green material.

00:46:34.520 --> 00:46:36.480
It kept glowing.

00:46:37.280 --> 00:46:44.680
This glimmering stuff has been found in dark nooks and crannies around the globe.

00:46:48.800 --> 00:46:49.960
'Wow!'

00:46:49.160 --> 00:46:51.600
In Cornwall...

00:46:56.440 --> 00:47:01.360
Germany...

00:47:00.720 --> 00:47:05.920
'Holy BLEEP, look at that down there.

00:47:04.960 --> 00:47:06.080
'It is, it's glowing.'

00:47:06.960 --> 00:47:08.120
And even New York state.

00:47:07.200 --> 00:47:11.920
So what on earth could be producing this eerie green glow?

00:47:11.760 --> 00:47:22.040
Well, some rocks have the ability to fluoresce, the minerals within them can absorb light and then re-emit it.

00:47:21.280 --> 00:47:22.840
Like glow-in-the-dark stickers.

00:47:24.760 --> 00:47:33.440
True, but could this glowing green goo actually be alive?

00:47:32.520 --> 00:47:39.840
In Africa, there is a fungus that lights up the forest floor.

00:47:39.040 --> 00:47:45.160
It uses the luminous enzymes to breakdown leaf litter.

00:47:44.360 --> 00:47:49.680
The locals call it chimpanzee fire.

00:47:50.560 --> 00:47:54.320
But there was something completely unfathomable about the green goo that Anthony found in Wales.

00:47:53.520 --> 00:47:59.120
It had the disturbing ability to vanish before his eyes.

00:47:59.160 --> 00:48:10.480
It wasn't until I took a few steps closer, looked back at it from a slightly different direction and realised it's completely disappeared.

00:48:09.560 --> 00:48:11.440
I couldn't see it at all.

00:48:12.120 --> 00:48:17.320
So what was this glistening substance that glowed one moment and was gone the next?

00:48:16.400 --> 00:48:24.960
In the past, people believed it was the treasure of goblins.

00:48:24.200 --> 00:48:34.960
Probably hundreds of years, the stories about goblins hiding their gold in caves and all sorts of dark places.

00:48:34.120 --> 00:48:44.000
People walking past dark caves and such places see this glow of emeralds in the back and they go in and say, "This is the treasure, we're going to go in..."

00:48:43.080 --> 00:48:47.880
And you go in and grab a bit and you come out, and of course, it's not there.

00:48:47.040 --> 00:48:48.320
It's completely gone.

00:48:47.440 --> 00:48:54.800
So, obviously, it's goblins, what else could it be?

00:48:57.200 --> 00:49:05.000
In fact, these aren't sparkling gems hidden by mythical creatures.

00:49:04.040 --> 00:49:05.280
But the answer is almost as magical.

00:49:04.360 --> 00:49:10.480
It's a very clever trick by a moss that lives in these special circumstances.

00:49:09.680 --> 00:49:16.080
A moss called goblin gold.

00:49:15.120 --> 00:49:16.200
But this is no ordinary moss.

00:49:15.240 --> 00:49:20.680
It's developed some very special cells that allow it to live where there's almost no light.

00:49:19.880 --> 00:49:23.840
So how does it work?

00:49:25.040 --> 00:49:40.600
The front of each cell is curved like a lens, this focuses light rays and boosts the amount of light reaching the chloroplasts - those parts of the plant that produce energy through photosynthesis.

00:49:41.520 --> 00:49:46.600
The reason it glows is that some of the light is reflected back out through that lens.

00:49:45.800 --> 00:49:51.440
And this is the key to the moss's mysterious disappearance.

00:49:53.240 --> 00:50:00.080
Those reflected light rays can only be seen from one particular angle.

00:49:59.120 --> 00:50:06.040
And Anthony happened to stop in just the right place.

00:50:05.120 --> 00:50:10.480
If I'd been one step higher or one step lower, I wouldn't have noticed it.

00:50:09.680 --> 00:50:14.440
Imagine that.

00:50:13.600 --> 00:50:21.480
Lurking out there in the dark, all across the UK, there's green gold just waiting to be discovered.

00:50:23.080 --> 00:50:24.840
Fantastic.

00:50:24.080 --> 00:50:27.600
For our final dose of weirdness, we head to southern Spain.

00:50:27.440 --> 00:50:35.480
Normally, Calasparra is a small, sleepy town.

00:50:35.680 --> 00:50:49.600
But in November 2015, a local farmer found something out of this world.

00:50:49.040 --> 00:50:52.480
A peculiar, alien object appeared out of nowhere.

00:50:54.560 --> 00:51:07.400
I saw a black ball about 60 centimetres across lying close to the bottom of the bank, and a big dent where it hit the ground.

00:51:06.840 --> 00:51:11.880
When I saw it, I called the police straightaway.

00:51:11.040 --> 00:51:17.480
They told me not to touch it.

00:51:17.040 --> 00:51:24.680
The police cautiously collected the huge hairy ball and took it away for analysis.

00:51:24.000 --> 00:51:28.080
And this wasn't a one-off.

00:51:27.240 --> 00:51:36.480
That month, two more of these eerie orbs turned up in the region.

00:51:35.760 --> 00:51:42.640
And around the globe, there were reports of more weird objects suddenly appearing.

00:51:41.840 --> 00:51:46.600
These odd balls turned up in Vietnam.

00:51:46.960 --> 00:51:51.000
Another was discovered in Brazil.

00:51:50.200 --> 00:51:51.360
But where had they all come from?

00:51:54.480 --> 00:51:56.880
Back in Spain, there was a clue.

00:51:58.640 --> 00:52:08.000
Witnesses in the town said they saw strange objects, like a cluster of fire, which then dispersed and fell.

00:52:10.640 --> 00:52:17.480
They told me there were between four and six unidentified flying objects.

00:52:16.680 --> 00:52:20.560
Burning UFOs falling from space.

00:52:19.760 --> 00:52:26.640
Understandably, the mayor was very concerned.

00:52:25.760 --> 00:52:33.800
We were worried that it might happen again, falling on a primary school, someone's home, or a playground.

00:52:33.120 --> 00:52:40.200
So what could explain these menacing missiles that tumbled to Earth?

00:52:39.520 --> 00:52:40.840
Whoa!

00:52:42.280 --> 00:52:47.480
Well, our skies are the scene of some strange sights.

00:52:48.520 --> 00:52:50.360
One possibility is that they were some sort of mysterious meteorite.

00:52:49.520 --> 00:52:57.760
This one caused confusion when it landed in Kenya.

00:52:57.880 --> 00:53:02.760
'Residents of the Ol Donyo Sabuk area hear a loud noise 'from the sky above.

00:53:02.840 --> 00:53:04.480
But there's no aircraft in sight.

00:53:03.600 --> 00:53:05.640
'Instead, a strange object, whose appearance left many puzzled.'

